+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers share tablets and sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes away from the server. A visit is a structured record — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes — submitted by an authenticated worker. The server can receive two edited versions of the same visit days apart, each built from the same starting state, with no indication from the network which reflects the "better" science.
+
+The record store must define what happens when two such submissions arrive. The choice here is foundational: it determines the submission envelope tablets send, the server-side conflict representation, the shape of coordinator review work, and which visits are eligible for the Friday CSV export. Tablet-sync and review-export cannot be designed until this contract is fixed.
+
+Two constraints bound the space. First, no approach may discard an original submission — this is a standing project tenet. Second, the record store cannot automatically infer all scientific field relationships from field names: a species identification code and its cover-class estimate are paired (changing one without the other may produce a scientifically invalid entry), and a worker who corrects a species code is doing different work from one who corrects an observer name, even though both edits touch different fields. Any mechanism that automatically combines field-level changes must account for relationships the server has no schema for.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **No submission discarded** — an approach that could silently lose an original submission is eliminated. Both approaches in contention preserve all submitted payloads.
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Scientific correctness risk** (major) — tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.* An automatically produced record must not introduce scientifically incorrect values without coordinator awareness. The cost of undetected incorrect data in the export is high: the science partner builds analyses on it.
+
+- **Preservation of worker intent as a unit** (major) — tenet: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit.* A visit record represents a complete field observation. When a worker submits a visit, they are asserting a coherent whole. An approach that decomposes or partially replaces that assertion changes what was submitted without the worker's involvement.
+
+- **Coordinator review volume** (major) — tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate.* Two coordinators handle all review work for roughly 6,000 visits per season. An approach that routes all concurrent edits to review, including edits to unrelated fields, imposes a larger queue than one that automatically handles non-conflicting cases.
+
+- **Implementation complexity** (moderate) — tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.* The server-side logic must be built and maintained. Complexity here compounds over time as the field schema evolves: rules added for current fields must be revisited when fields are added or changed.
+
+- **Tablet submission requirements** (moderate) — what each approach requires tablets to track and transmit shapes offline storage, retry logic, and the tablet-sync design.
+
+- **Review decision quality** (moderate) — coordinators need enough context to make accurate decisions. An approach that restricts the coordinator's view to a subset of fields may withhold context relevant to the scientific judgment.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Option A: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Every accepted submission creates an immutable revision record carrying the full visit payload and a `base_revision_id` pointing to the revision the submitter started from. The server compares each incoming `base_revision_id` to the visit's current accepted revision: if they match, the new revision linearly advances the current state; if they differ (or the visit is already in conflict), the new revision is added to the competing set and the visit becomes unresolved. Unresolved visits have two or more complete competing revisions.
+
+A coordinator reviews the competing set by seeing the full payload of each competing revision side by side, then selects one as the accepted revision or authors a manual combination. The visit becomes resolved. Only resolved visits enter the CSV export.
+
+**Tablets** track one `base_revision_id` per in-progress visit edit and include it in every submission. On receiving a response indicating a competing revision exists, the tablet surfaces this to the field worker.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Scientific correctness risk: **strong fit**. Every concurrent edit goes to coordinator review. No automatic combination happens, so no scientifically related fields are ever silently joined. The coordinator always sees both complete visit records before a combined state enters the export.
+- Preservation of worker intent as a unit: **strong fit**. Each revision is the worker's complete, intact submission. Neither the server nor the coordinator is required to decompose or partially merge it.
+- Coordinator review volume: **weak fit**. Any concurrent edit — including edits to entirely unrelated fields — creates a review item. A worker who corrects a typo in the observer list and another who adds a missing species both produce a competing revision, even though their edits do not interact. The queue grows with the frequency of any concurrent editing, not just scientifically meaningful conflicts.
+- Implementation complexity: **strong fit** (simpler). Server logic reduces to a single ID comparison. Tablets store one ID per in-progress edit. No field-type merge rules exist to write or maintain.
+- Tablet submission requirements: **strong fit** (lighter). Tablets submit full payloads and track one ID. No structured change-set computation on the device.
+- Review decision quality: **strong fit**. Coordinators see complete alternative records in full context. No information is withheld.
+
+*The essential trade: all concurrent edits, whether scientifically related or not, become coordinator work — in exchange for no automatic combination ever reaching the export.*
+
+### Option B: Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Every accepted submission includes the base snapshot the worker started from and a change set representing only the fields they modified. The server computes field-level diffs against the base and applies a three-way merge: fields changed by only one submitter are applied automatically; fields changed by both submitters are flagged for coordinator review. A visit with only disjoint field changes is resolved automatically; one with overlapping changes is unresolved.
+
+Merge rules are defined per field type: scalar fields (observation date) use last-writer-wins or flag overlap; observer lists use set-union for non-overlapping additions and flag removals or overlapping changes; species cover entries use the species code as key, with disjoint species entries combined automatically and same-species changes flagged.
+
+**Tablets** compute and transmit a structured change set (base snapshot plus field-level diffs) rather than a full payload. This requires change-tracking logic on the device. In offline scenarios, the tablet must retain the full base snapshot to construct the diff at submission time.
+
+**Analysis:**
+
+- Scientific correctness risk: **weak fit**. Automatically combined records bypass coordinator review. The merge rules operate on field names and types, not scientific relationships between fields. A worker who corrects a species identification code and another who corrects that species' cover estimate would see their changes automatically combined — producing a record that neither worker submitted, asserting a cover estimate against a species the first worker did not intend and an identification the second worker did not intend, with no coordinator seeing the combined record before it becomes the accepted state. Adding explicit rules for known field pairs (species code ↔ cover estimate) partially mitigates this but cannot cover relationships introduced by future schema changes or field usage patterns the rules were not written for.
+- Preservation of worker intent as a unit: **partial fit**. For non-overlapping changes, neither worker's complete visit is preserved as submitted; the accepted record is a server-constructed combination. For overlapping changes, the coordinator's view is field-scoped, without the surrounding visit context.
+- Coordinator review volume: **strong fit**. Disjoint field changes resolve automatically, creating no review items. Only genuinely overlapping edits enter the queue. Under typical usage — where most concurrent edits are to different fields — the queue is substantially smaller.
+- Implementation complexity: **weak fit** (more complex). Field-type merge rules must be authored, tested, and maintained as the visit schema evolves. A new field type requires a new merge rule; a changed field meaning may silently invalidate an existing one. Change-set computation moves to the tablet, requiring tested diff logic on the device.
+- Tablet submission requirements: **weak fit** (heavier). Tablets must retain the full base snapshot, compute structured change sets, and handle the case where the base snapshot has been updated server-side before the tablet reconnects.
+- Review decision quality: **partial fit**. Coordinators reviewing overlapping fields see only those fields, not the full visit context. Automatically combined fields are not shown at all, so a coordinator cannot catch a merge that is field-level clean but scientifically wrong.
+
+*The essential trade: most concurrent edits resolve without coordinator involvement, reducing review load substantially — in exchange for automatically combined records that may contain scientifically incorrect values the coordinator never sees.*
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions (Option A).**
+
+The decision turns on scientific correctness risk and preservation of worker intent, both major criteria. Field-aware merge's automatic combination produces accepted records no coordinator has seen, and the merge rules cannot reliably capture all scientifically meaningful field relationships — particularly species identification codes paired with their cover estimates. An undetected merge of related fields produces a record asserted as correct in the Friday export. That failure mode — exported incorrect data — is worse than a larger coordinator review queue.
+
+The larger queue (Option A's primary cost) is the direct consequence of treating the team's tenet seriously: the path from a submitted observation to an exported value must be inspectable, and an automatically combined record breaks that path. Two coordinators operating a review queue, however large, preserves it. A queue that has grown intolerable is a recoverable problem; incorrect exported science is not.
+
+Field-aware merge remains the credible alternative if operational experience shows the queue volume is genuinely unsustainable. Revisiting it would require the team to explicitly enumerate field pairs with known scientific relationships and accept that automatically combined fields for those pairs will bypass coordinator review. That is a decision about scientific risk tolerance the team should make consciously, not inherit from an implementation convenience.
+
+**What this forecloses:** the server never automatically combines field values from competing submissions. Every concurrent edit that shares a base creates coordinator work.
+
+**What this obligates downstream:**
+
+- *Tablet-sync* must track and persist `base_revision_id` per in-progress visit edit; must include it in every submission envelope; and must surface a "competing revision exists" state to field workers when the server returns that outcome.
+- *Review-export* must implement a revision comparison screen showing competing complete visit records side by side (the coordinator's primary resolution interface); must exclude all unresolved visits from the CSV export; and must expose the coordinator operations (select, author) defined by the record store's resolution contract.
